Error fixtures for the arith grammar of examples/generic-methods.
Each .error member is the output of `ohm-cli test match_error` on the
.txt member of the same test, and each .expected member the output of
`ohm-cli test match_error --expected`, and each .next_tokens member the
output of `ohm-cli test next_tokens`. These fixtures compile the grammar
with docker. See error-reporting prd section 10.

The text matches miniohm in the ohmjs/ohm:18.0.0-beta.15 docker image
(its runtime package.json says ohm-js 18.0.0-beta.13), run on a fresh
Grammar per input. miniohm passes ruleNames[0] (Exp) to recordFailures,
not the start rule, yet gives the same text for prim.start=PriExp.

Test paren replaces the plan's input `1 +\n (2 * x`, which traps in
recordFailures: a Wasm stack overflow here, and `Maximum call stack size
exceeded` in miniohm. See error-reporting prd section 0 and
docs/kb/ohm-failure-recording.md. Paren adds a `(` and a `)` to that
input. It does not trap, and has fluffy failures only with flag N; with
the trailing newline every failure is non-fluffy.

Test unclosed pins what the Go runtime does on the trap: Err() gives the
position only, so the message ends at the caret and the short message
reads `match failed`. miniohm throws instead. No unclosed.expected
member is possible: `test match_error --expected` fails with
`error calling recordFailures: stack overflow`, a processing error, and
so does `test next_tokens`. When the compiler is fixed, both unclosed
members must change.
-- grammar.ohm --
Arithmetic {
  Exp
    = AddExp

  AddExp
    = AddExp "+" MulExp  -- plus
    | AddExp "-" MulExp  -- minus
    | MulExp

  MulExp
    = MulExp "*" PriExp  -- times
    | MulExp "/" PriExp  -- divide
    | PriExp

  PriExp
    = "(" Exp ")"  -- paren
    | ident
    | number

  ident  (an identifier)
    = letter alnum*

  number  (a number)
    = digit+
}
-- bad_op.txt --
1 + * 2
-- bad_op.error --
Line 1, col 5:
> 1 | 1 + * 2
          ^
  2 | 
Expected "(", an identifier, or a number
-- bad_op.s.error --
Line 1, col 5: expected "(", an identifier, or a number
-- bad_op.expected --
literal "("
description an identifier
description a number
-- bad_op.next_tokens --
literal "(": accepted
description an identifier: untested
description a number: untested
-- trailing.txt --
abc 12
-- trailing.error --
Line 1, col 5:
> 1 | abc 12
          ^
  2 | 
Expected end of input
-- trailing.s.error --
Line 1, col 5: expected end of input
-- trailing.expected --
end end of input
-- trailing.next_tokens --
end end of input: match succeeded
-- empty.txt --
-- empty.error --
Line 1, col 1:
> 1 | 
      ^
Expected "(", an identifier, or a number
-- empty.s.error --
Line 1, col 1: expected "(", an identifier, or a number
-- empty.expected --
literal "("
description an identifier
description a number
-- prim.txt --
x +
-- prim.start=PriExp.error --
Line 1, col 3:
> 1 | x +
        ^
  2 | 
Expected end of input
-- prim.start=PriExp.s.error --
Line 1, col 3: expected end of input
-- prim.start=PriExp.expected --
end end of input
-- prim.start=PriExp.next_tokens --
end end of input: match succeeded
-- paren.txt --
(1 +
 (2 * x)
-- paren.N.error --
Line 2, col 9:
  1 | (1 +
> 2 |  (2 * x)
              ^
Expected ")"
-- paren.N.expected --
literal ")"
-- paren.N.f.expected --
literal "*" (fluffy)
literal "/" (fluffy)
literal "+" (fluffy)
literal "-" (fluffy)
literal ")"
-- paren.N.next_tokens --
literal ")": match succeeded
-- paren.N.f.next_tokens --
literal "*": accepted (fluffy)
literal "/": accepted (fluffy)
literal "+": accepted (fluffy)
literal "-": accepted (fluffy)
literal ")": match succeeded
-- unclosed.txt --
1 +
 (2 * x
-- unclosed.N.error --
Line 2, col 8:
  1 | 1 +
> 2 |  (2 * x
             ^
-- unclosed.N.s.error --
Line 2, col 8: match failed
-- ok.txt --
1 + 2 * (3 - x)
-- ok.error --
match succeeded
-- ok.s.error --
match succeeded
-- ok.expected --
match succeeded
-- ok.next_tokens --
match succeeded
